---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getLocaleFromUrl } from '@/i18n/utils';
import { getBlogPost, blogChrome } from '@/i18n/blog';

const locale = getLocaleFromUrl(Astro.url, Astro.currentLocale);
const post = getBlogPost('static-vs-dynamic-qr-codes', locale);
const chrome = blogChrome[locale];
---

<Base title={post.title} description={post.description} schema="faq" faqItems={post.faqItems}>
  <article class="blog-post">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">{chrome.home}</a> › <a href="/blog/">{chrome.blog}</a> › <span>{post.breadcrumb}</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="blog-header">
        <p class="blog-meta">{post.meta}</p>
        <h1>{post.h1}</h1>
        <p class="blog-lede">{post.lede}</p>
      </header>

      {post.bodyNote && (
        <p class="blog-body-note">{post.bodyNote}</p>
      )}

      <div class="blog-content">
        <p>If you've shopped for QR code generators, you've seen two prices: free, and "from $5/month." The free generators make <strong>static</strong> QR codes. The paid generators make <strong>dynamic</strong> QR codes. Understanding the difference saves you real money — and prevents a painful reprint when your URL changes.</p>

        <h2>Static QR codes: the data is the code</h2>
        <p>A static QR code encodes the destination directly in the pattern of black and white squares. The QR pattern <em>is</em> the URL. If you want to change the URL, you generate a brand-new QR code and replace every physical copy you printed.</p>
        <p>Pros:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Free, forever — no subscription needed</li>
          <li>No third-party server involved — works forever, even if the generator disappears</li>
          <li>Privacy: no scan tracking, no analytics, no data leaves the device</li>
          <li>Unlimited scans, no per-scan fees</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Cons:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Changing the destination means regenerating and re-printing</li>
          <li>No scan analytics (you can't tell how many people scanned, when, where)</li>
          <li>Can't personalize the destination per scan (e.g., per language or per user)</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Dynamic QR codes: the code is a redirect</h2>
        <p>A dynamic QR code encodes a short URL that points to the generator company's server. When someone scans, the server logs the scan, then redirects to whatever destination you've configured in the dashboard.</p>
        <p>Pros:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Change the destination anytime without reprinting</li>
          <li>Scan analytics: total scans, time, device, location, scans per code</li>
          <li>Schedule campaigns: show a different destination on weekdays vs. weekends</li>
          <li>A/B test different landing pages</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Cons:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Recurring cost (typically $5–$50/month)</li>
          <li>The redirect server is a single point of failure — if the company goes down or you stop paying, every printed code becomes dead</li>
          <li>Privacy concerns: every scan is logged on a third-party server</li>
          <li>Slight delay (50–200ms) due to the redirect</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Which one do you actually need?</h2>
        <p>For 90% of use cases, static is the right answer. Here's a quick decision rule:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Business card, packaging, restaurant menu, event ticket, WiFi access, static documentation link</strong> — use static. The URL is permanent, so the code is permanent.</li>
          <li><strong>Marketing campaign where you'll iterate landing pages, conference booth with time-sensitive offers, print ad where you want scan metrics</strong> — use dynamic, accept the ongoing cost.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The hidden cost of dynamic</h2>
        <p>The monthly fee is the obvious cost. The hidden cost is lock-in: once you've printed 10,000 cards or 50,000 packaging units with a dynamic code, you're committed to that provider. If they go out of business, raise prices, or get acquired, you're stuck. With a static code, you own the asset forever — even if the generator vanishes.</p>
        <p>This is also why many privacy-conscious users and businesses refuse to use dynamic codes at all: every scan is a tracking event, recorded on someone else's server, indefinitely.</p>

        <h2>When static is wrong</h2>
        <p>If your destination URL will genuinely change — say, you're running a flash sale and the URL needs to update weekly — dynamic saves you from reprinting 5,000 flyers. The math: $50/month × 12 months = $600/year. If reprinting would cost more than that, dynamic wins.</p>
        <p>But for permanent destinations (your business website, your contact card, your WiFi password), static is cheaper, more reliable, and more private.</p>

        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>Use <a href="/">our free generator</a> for static QR codes — no sign-up, no tracking, no expiration. Use a paid dynamic service only when you've calculated that the analytics and editability are worth the recurring cost and the third-party dependency.</p>

        <h2>{chrome.faqHeading}</h2>
        {post.faqItems.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.question}</summary>
            <p>{f.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}

        <p class="cta">Generate a static QR code — free, no sign-up, works forever. <a href="/">Make a QR code →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .blog-post { padding: var(--space-lg) 0; }
  .breadcrumbs { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-md); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-fg-soft); }
  .breadcrumbs a { color: var(--color-fg-soft); }
  .breadcrumbs a:hover { color: var(--color-accent-strong); }
  .blog-header { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-lg); text-align: center; }
  .blog-meta { color: var(--color-fg-soft); font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); }
  .blog-header h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; line-height: 1.2; }
  .blog-lede { font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--color-fg-soft); max-width: 640px; margin: var(--space-md) auto 0; }
  .blog-body-note {
    max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-md);
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    color: var(--color-accent-strong);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .blog-content { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .blog-content h2 { margin-top: var(--space-xl); }
  .blog-content h3 { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
  .blog-content p, .blog-content li { font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .blog-content details {
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0; padding: var(--space-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--color-bg) 0%, var(--color-bg-soft) 100%);
  }
  .blog-content summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; }
  .blog-content .cta { margin-top: var(--space-xl); padding: var(--space-md); background: var(--color-accent-soft); border-radius: var(--radius-md); text-align: center; }
  .blog-content .cta a { font-weight: 600; }
</style>
